#  WITHOUT ANY WARRANTY; without even the implied warranty of
#  MERCHANTABILITY or FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE. See the GNU
#  Lesser General Public License for more details.
#
#  You should have received a copy of the GNU Lesser General Public
#  License along with liblouis. If not, see
#  <http://www.gnu.org/licenses/>.
#
#  Remarks: this table is largely based following the conventions as set in the ONCE
#  international manual for contracted (abbreviated) Spanish grade 2 stenography manual
#  published in 1998, by Mª del Carmen Galíndez González, available at
#  https://www.foal.es/es/biblioteca/curso-de-estenograf%C3%AD. The official Spanish
#  dictionary (RAE 23rd edition, 2017 update) was used for the check up of several things,
#  including contraction combinations, to check whether words existed in the language and
#  so on. Found at http://dle.rae.es/. Revised accentuation rules are also in effect,
#  especially those that deal with pronouns. This is important to mention as it is likely
#  to confuse many people due to the recent revision and many still have misconceptions
#  about pronouns. In a nutshell the accent for all demonstrative pronouns is now obsolete
#  in the Spanish language, see:
#  http://www.rae.es/consultas/el-adverbio-solo-y-los-pronombres-demostrativos-sin-tilde. A
#  few other secondary sources as well as answers to numerous questions to skilled braille
#  readers in different blindness braille Spanish social media groups have also been used
#  for completeness. The listed reference is the latest dated one which had no conflicts
#  or inconsistencies that could be found. For example, another unpublished online
#  reference dated 2006 did exist in Colombia, however the entire text was already in
#  grade 2 form, rendering it rather useless. It's important to note that this table is
#  provided as a convenience only since there are currently (present year 2018) no
#  official editorials or organizations which offer Spanish grade 2 texts or
#  books. However, the original creator feels that abreviated forms of reading and writing
#  are a key asset in any language, if not at least for personal use. Due that there are
#  some officially published sources in Spanish grade 2 it would be a shame to let all of
#  those world concerted efforts disappear and never make its way into modern times or
#  technology. Even though commercially renowned packages like Duxbury do support
#  abbreviated Spanish, various tests and readers have found it outdated and not always
#  corrrectly implemented even as of version 12.3 in present year 2018. Open sourcing the
#  grade 2 code will hopefully prevent this from happening and will keep braille moving
#  forward. Usual disclaimer: of course, everyone who rigorously tested the table and
#  improved its opcodes, etc. has done their best so that rules and signs are represented
#  clearly and accurately, but cannot accept any responsibility for any errors or problems
#  the user may encounter in applying it to his or her work. So reporting of any of those
#  is always encouraged.
#
#  Update (2025): the abbreviations, word beginnings (principles), endings, sequences,
#  contractions and the use of dot 5 follow the current official reference, published by
#  the Comision Braille Espanola (ONCE): Documento tecnico B 16-1, "Estenografia espanola
#  y criterios didacticos para su aprendizaje", version 3, October 2025. Found at
#  https://www.once.es/servicios-sociales/braille/comision-braille-espanola/documentos-tecnicos/documentos-tecnicos-relacionados-con-el-braille/documentos-tecnicos-b-16-estenografia
#  Section numbers of that document are given in the comments of the rules.
#
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------

# Add virtual dot to k and w (see below). This overwrites the definitions from
# es-g1.ctb. For the table to work these rules need to be defined before AND after the
# include.
lowercase k 13b
lowercase w 2456b

include es-g1.ctb

# braille indicators specific to Spanish Grade 2

# grade 2 interruptor and also accentuation indicator

multind 56 letsign capsletter 

# emphasis start and end

emphclass italic
emphclass underline
emphclass bold

begemph bold 5-35
endemph bold 5-35
begemph italic 5-35
endemph italic 5-35

# Contractions made of signs in the lower part of the cell

# These are used like any other contraction, also in words made up of nothing but such
# contractions (Documento tecnico B 16-1, version 3, section 3.4.2: tren, trinen, drenen).

always ab 2
always br 23
always cr 25
always dr 256
always en 26
always in 35
always er 236
always gr 2356
always pr 235
always tr 356

# Spanish grade 2 situations solved with the use of multi-pass implementations

# Whenever w or k is anywhere in a word the entire word is uncontracted and dot 5 prefixes the word
# (Documento tecnico B 16-1, version 3, 4.2.1). The letter x does not interrupt contractions.

# Translate words containing k or w according to character definitions.

nocont K
nocont W
nocont k
nocont w

# To prefix the words with dot 5 we use a multi-pass implementation. First we add a virtual
# dot to k and w so that they can be distinguished in pass2.
lowercase k 13b
lowercase w 2456b

# Then we define a class and a swap set. The class is needed to make the negations work.
# (does not work with only the swapdd rule: is this a bug?)
attribute kw kw # this is the 1st class defined in this table, so we can also use $w instead of %kw
# The swap set is needed for the rule that removes the virtual dots.
swapdd kw 13b,2456b 13,2456
# We are using the same name for both because we can.

# In a first pass we add the dot 5.
noback pass2 _$s[%kw] @5*
noback pass2 _$s[!$ws.%kw] @5* # !$ws means "anything but a K, W or SPACE"
noback pass2 `[%kw] @5*
noback pass2 `[!$ws.%kw] @5*

# In a second pass we remove the virtual dots.
noback pass3 %kw %kw

# Dot 5 (Documento tecnico B 16-1, version 3, 4.2) separates a punctuation or expression sign
# from a stenographic sign that is written with the same cell.

# The closing question mark, quotation marks and special typography always carry it when they
# are the only closing sign.
endword ?" 5-26-236
endword " 5-236
endword ? 5-26
endword » 5-236
endword ” 5-236

# The rules above do not apply inside a word with k or w (it is not contracted, see above), so
# the closing question mark gets its dot 5 here. The pattern needs a letter before the sign and
# a space, a closing sign or the end of the text after it.
noback context $l["?"]$s @5-26
noback context $l["?"]~ @5-26
noback context $l["?"]"\"" @5-26
noback context $l["?"]"»" @5-26
noback context $l["?"]"”" @5-26

# An opening sign in front of a word in lower case: dot 5 before the sign. In front of a
# capital letter or a number the sign for those separates it. With several opening signs the
# dot 5 goes before the last one.
noback context ["¿"]$u @5-26
noback context ["¡"]$u @5-235
noback context ["«"]$u @5-236
noback context ["“"]$u @5-236
noback context ["("]$u @5-126
noback context $s["–"]$u @5-36
noback context `["–"]$u @5-36
noback context $s["\""]$u @5-236
noback context `["\""]$u @5-236

# The asterisk of an erroneous expression (Table 23, rule 1) is written 256 like the beginning
# dis-, so it takes dot 5 before it: *cocretas, *distrezas.
noback context ["*"]$u @5-256

# An opening sign before the name of a country: the sign 456 stands for the capital sign, so
# the opening sign is separated with dot 5 (4.2.2.1: «Portugal»).
noback pass2 @236@46@7 @5@236
noback pass2 @236@46@46@7 @5@236
noback pass2 @26@46@7 @5@26
noback pass2 @26@46@46@7 @5@26
noback pass2 @235@46@7 @5@235
noback pass2 @235@46@46@7 @5@235
noback pass2 @126@46@7 @5@126
noback pass2 @126@46@46@7 @5@126
noback pass2 @0@36@46@7 @0@5@36
noback pass2 @0@36@46@46@7 @0@5@36

# The same for the names made of several words, once the second pass has written their sign.
noback pass3 @236@456@14@1235 @5@236@456@14@1235
noback pass3 @236@456@234@1236 @5@236@456@234@1236
noback pass3 @236@456@145@135 @5@236@456@145@135
noback pass3 @26@456@14@1235 @5@26@456@14@1235
noback pass3 @26@456@234@1236 @5@26@456@234@1236
noback pass3 @26@456@145@135 @5@26@456@145@135
noback pass3 @235@456@14@1235 @5@235@456@14@1235
noback pass3 @235@456@234@1236 @5@235@456@234@1236
noback pass3 @235@456@145@135 @5@235@456@145@135
noback pass3 @126@456@14@1235 @5@126@456@14@1235
noback pass3 @126@456@234@1236 @5@126@456@234@1236
noback pass3 @126@456@145@135 @5@126@456@145@135
noback pass3 @0@36@456@14@1235 @0@5@36@456@14@1235
noback pass3 @0@36@456@234@1236 @0@5@36@456@234@1236
noback pass3 @0@36@456@145@135 @0@5@36@456@145@135

# One dot 5 is enough when the word has its own (qué, kilómetro).
noback pass2 @5@26@5 @26@5
noback pass2 @5@235@5 @235@5
noback pass2 @5@236@5 @236@5
noback pass2 @5@126@5 @126@5
noback pass2 @5@36@5 @36@5
noback pass3 @5@5@26 @26@5
noback pass3 @5@5@235 @235@5
noback pass3 @5@5@236 @236@5
noback pass3 @5@5@126 @126@5

# With several closing signs the dot 5 goes before the first one, also when it does not
# usually carry it (exclamation mark, parenthesis). A sign that is repeated is not another one.
attribute closeq ?)»”"
attribute closee ?!»”"
noback context ["!"]%closeq @5-235
noback context [")"]%closee @5-345

# A semicolon after a word that ends in -bre and a comma after a word that ends in ab
# (baobab, octubre;) would be read as part of the word.
noback pass2 @23[@23]$s @5-23
noback pass2 @23[@23]~ @5-23
noback pass2 @2[@2]$s @5-2
noback pass2 @2[@2]~ @5-2

